Q: Can&#39;t get an XML attribute from XML using jquery A: <p>Inside the loop, <code>$(this)</code> is still the whole XML, not just the current <code>&lt;geo&gt;</code> node. You should loop over the nodes to process them individually.</p>
<p>If you use <code>.each()</code> to loop over the nodes, it will set <code>this</code> to the current node in the iteration.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const xml = `&lt;markers&gt;
  &lt;marker&gt;
   &lt;name&gt;Company A&lt;/name&gt;
   &lt;geo comp="HQ"&gt;44.42, 26.95&lt;/geo&gt;  
   &lt;geo comp="SA plant"&gt;45.30, 21.88&lt;/geo&gt;
  &lt;/marker&gt;
  &lt;marker&gt;
&lt;name&gt;Company B&lt;/name&gt;
   &lt;geo comp="HQ"&gt;54.42, 66.95&lt;/geo&gt;  
  &lt;/marker&gt;
&lt;/markers&gt;`;

$(xml).find('geo').each(function() {
  let text = $(this).text();
  let [lat, long] = text.split(',');
  let sub = $(this).attr('comp');
  console.log(sub, lat, long);
});</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.7.1/jquery.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Pass and return the 3rd argument from argv A: <p>argv is an array of pointers. When you pass an array to a function, it basically degrades to a pointer of whatever type it was.  So an array of pointers degrades to a &quot;pointer to a pointer&quot;</p>
<p>Change your code to be:</p>
<pre><code>char const filename(char const **argv)
{
    char const *filename = argv[2];
    printf(&quot;File name is: %s\n&quot;, filename);
    return *filename;
}

int main(int argc, char const *argv[])
{
    filename(argv);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Loop Not Printing Multiple Rows (C, VS Code) A: <p>In analyzing this issue, there a numerous directions to address this issue, including performing bitwise manipulation.  In order to stay as closely to the spirit of the code noted above, following is one possible path to refactor the code and provide the &quot;character to byte&quot; representation outcome that is desired, taking into account the good comments provided.</p>
<p>As noted, the current hexadecimal calculation is being printed in a reverse order, which means a different perspective on how to work through the value of each character.  With that in mind, following is a refactored version of the code to provide the evaluation and printing of the appropriate '0' or '1' digit for a given ASCII character.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

#define SIZE 100
#define DIV  128

int main()
{
    char work[SIZE], c;
    int divisor;

    printf(&quot;Enter your message: &quot;);

    if (fgets(work, SIZE -1, stdin) == NULL)                    /* Don't have CS50 installed - use customary input function */
        return 1;

    printf(&quot;Binary representaton\n&quot;);

    for (int i = 0; (work[i] != '\0' &amp;&amp; work[i] != '\n'); i++)  /* Do until end of string terminator or newline character   */
    {
        divisor = DIV;
        c = work[i];

        for (int j = 0; j &lt; 8; j++)
        {
            printf(&quot;%d&quot;,(c - c % divisor) / divisor);           /* Subtract modulo of divisor from current value            */
            c = c % divisor;                                    /* Derive next value to evaluate                            */
            divisor /= 2;                                       /* Reduce divisor value by a half                           */
        }
        printf(&quot;\n&quot;);
    }

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The main things to take away from this refactored code are as follows:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Instead of a straight division or modulo operation using the value of &quot;2&quot;, a divisor value is initialized to a value of &quot;128&quot;, used in the modulo and division operations, and then subsequently divided by &quot;2&quot; through each loop iteration.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The current modulo value becomes the next value to be evaluated for the determination whether the calculated digit will be a '0' or a '1'.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>This process provides the &quot;left to right&quot; digit derivation.  Testing this with the character array value of &quot;Hi!&quot; noted in the above narrative provides the expected values.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Binary/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Binary 
Enter your message: Hi!
Binary representaton
01001000
01101001
00100001
</code></pre>
<p>To reiterate, there are numerous ways to address this issue, and this just happens to be one way to do it.  The takeaway here would be to delve into some &quot;C&quot; tutorials that deal with character evaluation, modulo operations, bitwise shifting, and hexadecimal representation.</p>
